  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Support of the 2022 Iraq Crisis Response Plan and Prioritize Funding for Support of the Displacement Tracking Matrix</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The crisis in Iraq saw the occupation of large areas of the country by Islamic State and the Levant (ISIL) followed by Government-led military operations to re-take ISIL controlled areas. The armed conflict led to massive internal displacement, the spread and scale of the violence resulted in country-wide and even regional instability. This has meant that it is essential for the Government of Iraq (GoI), the Kurdistan Regional Government (KRI), and the international humanitarian actors to have access to accurate, timely and comprehensive data on displacement in Iraq. The scale of displacement across all regions of Iraq has limited the ability of the government and humanitarian actors to effectively track and monitor ongoing population movements, necessary to identify, analyze and prioritize needs of those affected by the Iraq displacement crisis. The objective is to provide access to up-to-date, reliable and comprehensive information about internally displaced persons and returnee populations in Iraq, as well as their needs and vulnerabilities. This is achieved through the implementation of IOM&apos;s Displacement Tracking Matrix (DTM) tool and timely dissemination of results to government authorities, UN Humanitarian Country Team (UNHCT) partners, non-governmental organisation, think tanks, media, academics, and other stakeholders.</narrative>
  </description>
